---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';

const title = 'My Trades';
const description =
	'The trade Truth-Poly made on the Fed Chair market, and how the pipeline works.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
		<style>
			main {
				width: 720px;
				max-width: calc(100% - 2em);
				margin: auto;
				padding: 1em;
				text-align: left;
			}
			.notice {
				margin-top: 2em;
				padding: 1em;
				border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
				background: rgb(var(--gray-light));
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<h1>My Trades</h1>
			<p>
				This page explains the trade Truth-Poly made and how the pipeline works. The full code is
				on <a href="https://github.com/miagavin/truth-poly-ise">GitHub</a>.
			</p>

			<h2>The Kevin Warsh trade</h2>
			<p>
				At the end of January 2026, Donald Trump announced on Truth Social that Kevin Warsh was his
				pick for Fed Chair. Truth-Poly saw the post, worked out that it was a nomination, and bought
				Yes on Warsh in Polymarket's Fed Chair market at about 95 cents a share.
			</p>
			<p>
				A Yes share pays $1.00 if the outcome is Yes, so buying at 95 cents is a return of about
				5%. A few minutes later the price settled around 99.7 cents, once slower traders had
				reacted to the announcement.
			</p>

			<h2>Why there was an edge</h2>
			<p>
				I found the edge by comparing two timestamps: when Trump posted about calling off tariffs
				on European countries, and when the market actually moved. The market took a minute or more
				to react, because the traders who made money were doing it by hand. Truth-Poly acts within
				2-3 seconds of a post, so it can trade before the price catches up.
			</p>

			<h2>How the pipeline works</h2>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<strong>Poll.</strong> It checks Trump's Truth Social account every 1000ms.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Filter.</strong> Each post goes through a keyword filter, so only posts that
					might matter go further.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Classify.</strong> Claude reads the post and answers with the nominee's name, or
					"No nomination".
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Route.</strong> A router maps the name to Polymarket markets and buys the right
					asset. A malformed or unclear answer never becomes a trade.
				</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				Each step has its own post on the <a href="/blog">blog</a>, and the code is on
				<a href="https://github.com/miagavin/truth-poly-ise">GitHub</a>.
			</p>

			<p class="notice">
				<strong>Not financial advice.</strong> This is a personal project and a learning log.
				Prediction markets can lose you money; never risk more than you can afford to lose.
			</p>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>